exercises.solutionCode:
const http = require('http');
const fs = require('fs/promises');

const server = http.createServer(async (req, res) => {
  if (req.url === '/report') {
    const data = await fs.readFile('./big-report.csv');
    res.end(`Report size: ${data.length} bytes`);
  } else {
    res.end('OK');
  }
});

server.listen(3000);

sections.body:
Long before Promises existed in JavaScript, Node needed a convention for handing back the result of an asynchronous operation, and it settled on a rule followed across the core API and the ecosystem for years: **every callback's first parameter is reserved for an error**, and the remaining parameters carry the result. By convention you call them (err, data), and the first thing you do inside the callback is check whether err is truthy:

```javascript
const fs = require('fs');

fs.readFile('./config.json', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
  if (err) {
    console.error('Failed to read config:', err.message);
    return;
  }
  console.log('Config contents:', data);
});
```

The convention exists for a reason beyond style. With a synchronous function you wrap a risky call in try { ... } catch (err) { ... }, which works because the call stack containing your try block is still alive when the error is thrown. An asynchronous callback doesn't have that: by the time fs.readFile finishes and is ready to report, the original call to fs.readFile(...) has long since returned, and the stack frame containing any try/catch you wrapped around it is gone. An error thrown later would have nowhere to be caught by that original code. So Node's core APIs *pass* the error as data, as the first argument to the one function guaranteed to run when the operation completes: your callback. Skipping the if (err) check is the most common way bugs slip through error-first callback code, because it doesn't crash anything; the code proceeds to use data, which may be undefined or garbage.

Chain a few dependent asynchronous steps with callbacks (read a file, use its contents to query a database, use that result to write another file) and you get callbacks nested inside callbacks, called 'callback hell' or the 'pyramid of doom':

```javascript
fs.readFile('./user-id.txt', 'utf8', (err, id) => {
  if (err) return handleError(err);
  db.getUser(id.trim(), (err, user) => {
    if (err) return handleError(err);
    db.getOrders(user.id, (err, orders) => {
      if (err) return handleError(err);
      fs.writeFile('./orders.json', JSON.stringify(orders), (err) => {
        if (err) return handleError(err);
        console.log('Done.');
      });
    });
  });
});
```

The indentation is the visible symptom, but the costs run deeper. First, error handling has to be repeated correctly at every level: miss one if (err) return ... anywhere in the chain and the error at that step is swallowed while execution falls through to code that assumes success. Second, the shape is hard to *compose*: there's no simple way to say 'run these three independent lookups concurrently and continue once all three finish' with raw callbacks. You'd hand-roll a counter of how many of N parallel callbacks have fired, and handle more than one failing. Third, it's hard to *reuse*: you can't easily extract 'the middle three steps' into a named function and pass it around the way you can with a value. Promises, and later async/await, were designed to solve these three problems, not just to look prettier.

async/await is syntax sugar on top of Promises. It introduces no new concurrency mechanism, and thinking of it as 'Promises with different punctuation' will save you confused debugging. Marking a function async guarantees two things: it always returns a Promise (even if you write return 5;, callers get a Promise that resolves to 5, not the number 5), and inside its body you can use await in front of any Promise-returning expression. await somePromise pauses that async function at that line until the Promise settles, then either gives back the resolved value (as if it were a plain synchronous expression) or throws the rejection reason, which means you can use ordinary try/catch:

```javascript
async function processUser() {
  try {
    const id = await fs.readFile('./user-id.txt', 'utf8');
    const user = await db.getUser(id.trim());
    const orders = await db.getOrders(user.id);
    await fs.writeFile('./orders.json', JSON.stringify(orders));
    console.log('Done.');
  } catch (err) {
    handleError(err);
  }
}
```

This is the same four-step pipeline as the Promise chain above, written to read like ordinary sequential code. Two mistakes show up constantly once people start using await. First: **forgetting await.** const user = db.getUser(id); (missing await) waits for nothing: user is immediately bound to a *Promise object*, not the eventual user data, and code that reads user.id gets undefined or throws right away, because the Promise hasn't resolved yet. Worse, if that un-awaited Promise later rejects and nothing handles it, Node reports an 'unhandled promise rejection', and since Node 15 that **crashes the process** by default instead of just logging a warning. Second: **accidentally serializing work that could run in parallel.** Writing await inside a loop runs every iteration one after another, each finishing before the next starts, which is often not what you want if the operations are independent:

```javascript
// Serial: each request waits for the previous one to finish first
for (const id of userIds) {
  const user = await db.getUser(id);   // waits here, every single time
  results.push(user);
}

// Parallel: all requests fire together, we wait for all of them at once
const results = await Promise.all(userIds.map(id => db.getUser(id)));
```

Both versions are valid async/await code with no missing awaits. The difference is whether you intended each iteration to depend on the previous one, a decision that await-in-a-loop makes for you by default, often unintentionally. (Promise.all rejects as soon as any one promise rejects, and the others keep running, so use Promise.allSettled when you need every outcome.)

